---
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro'
import '../../styles/tools.css'
import { categories, crossReferenced, sources, tools, used, roles, usedPending, generated, bySlug } from '../../lib/tools'

const detail = (slug: string) => `/tools/${slug}/`
const hasPage = (slug: string) => {
  const t = bySlug.get(slug)
  return !!t && (t.refs.length > 1 || t.mentions.length > 0 || t.mentionedBy.length > 0)
}
const edges = tools.reduce((n, t) => n + t.mentions.length, 0)
---
<Doc
  title="Tools"
  description={`Two lists kept apart: ${used.length} tools with evidence that this site is built with them, and ${tools.length} catalogued from three public lists at a pinned commit.`}>

  <div class="mast">
    <div>Off the Bit
<b>Tools</b>
Category: Catalogue
Count: {used.length} with evidence, {tools.length} catalogued</div>
    <div class="r"><b>M. Imran</b>
Index: <a href="/">draft</a>
Sources: 3 lists</div>
  </div>

  <div class="title">
    <h1>Tools</h1>
    <div class="sub">Two lists, kept apart on purpose. One is short and carries evidence. The other is long and carries citations.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="ind">
    <div class="memo">The difference between these two halves is the only thing on this page worth
      being careful about. <b>Used here</b> is {used.length} tools, and every row names the file in
      this repository that proves it. <b>Catalogued</b> is {tools.length} tools parsed out of three
      public lists at a pinned commit: it records what those lists say, and it does not record that
      anyone here has opened any of them. Merging the two would produce a claim of fluency across
      six hundred products, which nobody has.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="ind tsec">
    <h2 id="find">Find</h2>
    <form class="tfind" method="get" action="/tools/search">
      <label class="vh" for="q">Search the catalogue</label>
      <input id="q" name="q" type="search" placeholder="postgres, terminal, code review" autocomplete="off" required>
      <button type="submit">Search</button>
    </form>
    <p class="dim" style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:12px;margin:2px 0 0">
      Searches names and descriptions. Answered server-side, because the reading path here ships
      no JavaScript and a search box that needs a bundle to work is a search box this site cannot have.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ind tsec">
    <h2 id="used">Used here</h2>
    <p class="prose">These build, check or serve this site. The evidence column is the point: each
      one names a file you can open in the repository and confirm, rather than asking you to take a
      rating on trust.</p>
    <ul class="tl">
      {used.map((u) => (
        <li>
          <div class="h">
            <a href={u.url} rel="noopener">{u.name}</a>
            <span class="trole">{u.role}</span>
          </div>
          {u.note && <div class="d">{u.note}</div>}
          <div class="tev"><b>Evidence:</b> {u.evidence}</div>
          {u.catalogue && hasPage(u.catalogue) && (
            <div class="m"><a href={detail(u.catalogue)}>also in the catalogue</a></div>
          )}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="prose"><b>What the three words mean.</b>
      {Object.entries(roles).map(([k, v]) => (<><br /><b>{k}</b> {v}</>))}</p>
    <p class="prose dim">{usedPending}</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ind tsec">
    <h2 id="categories">By category</h2>
    <p class="prose">Sixty-eight headings across the three source lists, collapsed into fourteen.
      The mapping is written out in <span class="kw">scripts/ingest-tools.mjs</span> rather than
      guessed from keywords, so a heading renamed upstream fails the ingest instead of quietly
      refiling a hundred tools.</p>
    <div class="tcats">
      {categories.map((c) => (
        <a class="tcat" href={`/tools/in/${c.key}/`}>
          <span class="n">{c.tools.length}</span>
          <b>{c.label}</b>
          <span class="b">{c.blurb}</span>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="ind tsec">
    <h2 id="most">Carried by more than one list</h2>
    <p class="prose">Whether a second list bothered to include a tool is the only popularity signal
      these sources contain. It is weak. It is also real, which is more than can be said for a star
      count copied off a badge, and it is the honest answer to "what is most used" from data that
      does not record use. {crossReferenced.length} of {tools.length} tools clear it.</p>
    <ul class="tl">
      {crossReferenced.map((t) => (
        <li>
          <div class="h">
            {hasPage(t.slug)
              ? <a href={detail(t.slug)}>{t.name}</a>
              : <a href={t.url} rel="noopener">{t.name}</a>}
            <span class="tref">{t.refs.length} of 3 lists</span>
          </div>
          <div class="d">{t.desc}</div>
          <div class="m"><a href={`/tools/in/${t.category}/`}>{t.category}</a></div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="ind tsec">
    <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
    <p class="prose">Read at the commit named below, not at whatever those repositories say today.
      The ingest runs by hand and its output is committed, because a build that reaches the network
      is a build whose result depends on a stranger's uptime. Descriptions are quoted from the list
      that supplied them; all three licences permit it and two require the attribution that this
      page carries.</p>
    <ul class="tsrc">
      {sources.map((s) => (
        <li><b><a href={s.url} rel="noopener">{s.title}</a></b><br />
          {s.entries} entries, read at <span class="kw">{s.commitShort}</span> of {s.commitDate}.
          Licence: <a href={s.licenceUrl} rel="noopener">{s.licence}</a>.</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="prose dim">Ingested {generated}. The oldest source has not been touched since
      {' '}{sources.map((s) => s.commitDate).sort()[0]}, so treat its rows as a snapshot of that
      date rather than as current.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ind tsec">
    <h2 id="graph">On the word "cloud"</h2>
    <p class="prose">This was asked for as a cloud, with every tool visibly wired to the ones it
      relates to. The data does not support that drawing, and the honest thing is to say so on the
      page rather than to invent the wiring.</p>
    <p class="prose">An edge here means one tool's description names another tool in the catalogue.
      That yields {edges} edges across {tools.length} tools, and eleven of the fourteen categories
      contain no internal edges at all. The reason is structural: a curated list describes each
      product on its own terms, so the text almost never mentions what a thing integrates with.
      Where a graph exists it is drawn on the category page. Where it does not, that page says so
      and lists the tools instead. A hundred nodes wired by shared category would look like a cloud
      and would mean nothing.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="foot"><span>Imran</span><span>Tools</span><span>[Catalogue]</span></div>
</Doc>
